=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The BIND 9 (`named`) DNS daemon on `srv1` (10.5.0.4) — the sole campus-wide resolver configured on all hosts, including `laptop1`.

**Mechanism:** `srv1`'s `/etc/bind/named.conf` contains `recursion no;` in the `options` block. With recursion disabled, BIND will only answer queries for zones it is authoritative for — in this case, solely `jeremysitlab.com`. Any query for a name outside that zone (bare hostnames such as `srv1`, `pc2`, or any internet FQDN) returns a DNS `REFUSED` response with the RA (Recursion Available) bit cleared. Because no host's `/etc/resolv.conf` includes a `search jeremysitlab.com` directive, clients send bare-name queries that never match the authoritative zone, so every single hostname lookup fails.

**Localization:** `srv1` — eth0 at 10.5.0.4/24. The named process (pid 30) is alive and listening on port 53; no `tc` qdisc delay is present; `allow-query { any; }` is set. The broken behaviour is exclusively `recursion no;` in the options stanza.

**Root cause:** BIND on `srv1` is configured with `recursion no;`, rendering it unable to resolve any hostname that is not a FQDN within `jeremysitlab.com`, which is effectively every query issued by campus clients. All hosts are affected; the laptop user's total connectivity failure is because every application-layer connection relies on name resolution.

**Fix:** Edit `/etc/bind/named.conf` on `srv1`, change `recursion no;` to `recursion yes;` (add `allow-recursion { any; };` if the default ACL is too restrictive), then run `rndc reload` or `systemctl restart bind9` to apply the change.
=== END ===